.TH MITOS-BOX 1 "September 2026" "mitos-utils 0.1.0" "MITOS User Commands"
.SH NAME
mitos\-box \- run any mitos\-utils applet from a single binary
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mitos\-box
\fIAPPLET\fR [\fIARGUMENT\fR...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
mitos\-box hosts every mitos\-utils applet (see mitos\-utils\-compat(7)
for the full list) in one binary, busybox/toybox\-style, so a mitosOS
install can carry one binary plus symlinks instead of a separate copy
of the runtime per utility.
.PP
Invoked as a symlink or hardlink named after an applet (e.g.
.B ln \-s mitos\-box cat
then running
.BR ./cat ),
mitos\-box reads
.B argv[0]
and dispatches straight to that applet, exactly as if the standalone
binary had been run.
.PP
Invoked directly with the applet name as the first argument
(\fBmitos\-box ls \-la\fR), it dispatches the same way without
needing a symlink. With no arguments, or an unrecognized applet name,
it lists every available applet.
.SH EXIT STATUS
Same as the dispatched applet. 2 if the requested applet name isn't
recognized. 1 if argv[0] couldn't be determined at all.
.SH SEE ALSO
See each applet's own man page (e.g.
.BR cat (1),
.BR ls (1))
for its specific options; they apply identically whether run standalone
or through mitos\-box.
.BR mitos\-utils\-integration (7)
